 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 529743,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<p><img src=\"exercises/media/991ea07b157d890e.jpg\" alt=\"Illustrated side view of bee showing labeled leaders to areas of hearts, ostia (openings in heart) on the dorsal aspect (top) of bee body, blood vessel running across top of bee body, and body cavity labeled on bottom of bee body.\"></p>\n\n<p>This diagram shows how insects have a heart that is elongated throughout much of their body. This is opposed to the heart of vertebrates, such as humans, which does not spread throughout the length of the body. Why was it likely advantageous for insects to evolve an elongated heart as opposed to the centrally located heart of vertebrates?</p>\n",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1036908,
     "content_html": "Because an elongated heart allows blood to easily reach all the parts of the body from the heart."
    },
    {
     "id": 1036909,
     "content_html": "Because the elongated heart allows more blood to reach all the parts of the body, it allows for more nutrient exchange."
    },
    {
     "id": 1036910,
     "content_html": "Because an elongated heart allows blood to easily return to the heart from a few points within the body cavity."
    },
    {
     "id": 1036911,
     "content_html": "Because an elongated heart allows blood to easily return to the heart from most points within the body cavity."
    }
   ],
